**Function: Transferrin Receptor Protein 1 (TFRC / CD71) — the principal cellular iron-uptake receptor.**
**Function: Transferrin Receptor Protein 1 (TFRC / CD71) — the principal cellular iron-uptake receptor.**

This 760-residue protein is unambiguously identified as human transferrin receptor 1 (100% identity to UniProt P02786). It is a **single-pass type II transmembrane glycoprotein** with the following architecture, confirmed by independent evidence:

- **Short N-terminal cytoplasmic tail** (residues 1–~56) containing the canonical **YTRF endocytosis/internalization motif**, which drives clathrin-mediated internalization.
- **One transmembrane helix** (~residues 57–77, the hydrophobic `IAVIVFFLIGFMIGYLGYCK` segment) anchoring the protein in the plasma membrane.
- **Large C-terminal ectodomain** (~residues 80–760) containing the three signature TfR1 domains: a **PA (protease-associated) domain** (~228–310), an **M28-peptidase-like fold** (~388–576) that is catalytically inactive, and a **TFR_dimer dimerization domain** (~638–749) that mediates homodimerization. The ectodomain is cysteine-rich with multiple disulfide bonds and N-glycosylation sites.

**Biological role:** TFRC mediates cellular iron uptake by binding iron-loaded transferrin (holo-Tf) at the cell surface with high affinity (as a homodimer), then internalizing it via **receptor-mediated endocytosis**. In the acidic endosome, iron is released; the apotransferrin–receptor complex is recycled to the surface where neutral pH releases apotransferrin for another cycle. Beyond iron delivery, TFRC:
- binds the hereditary hemochromatosis protein **HFE**, competing with transferrin and regulating systemic iron homeostasis;
- positively regulates T- and B-cell proliferation via iron supply;
- acts as a **lipid (stearate C18:0) sensor** that modulates JNK signaling and mitochondrial fusion (via MFN2 stability);
- serves as the **entry receptor for New World arenaviruses** (Junín, Machupo, Guanarito);
- is a classic marker of proliferating cells and is used clinically as a target for cancer therapy/imaging (e.g., anti-CD71 strategies).

In short, this is **transferrin receptor 1 (CD71)**, a homodimeric type II membrane receptor governing cellular iron homeostasis through transferrin-mediated endocytosis and recycling, with additional roles in immunity, lipid sensing/mitochondrial dynamics, and microbial/viral pathogenesis.

**Protein: CD16a — Low-affinity immunoglobulin gamma Fc region receptor III-A (FcγRIIIa; UniProt P08637)**
**Function:** CD16a is a **low-affinity receptor for the constant (Fc) region of immunoglobulin G (IgG)**. It does not bind free monomeric IgG, avoiding spurious activation; instead, it is optimally engaged by **clustered antigen–IgG immune complexes** on opsonized target cells. Upon ligation it activates ITAM-coupled signaling (PI3K, calcium flux, PKC), which:
- drives **antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC)** — lysis of antibody-coated infected or tumor cells;
- triggers **NK cell degranulation** and **pro-inflammatory cytokine production** (IFN-γ, TNF-α);
- supports **NK cell survival and proliferation** and generation of memory-like adaptive NK cells;
- mediates **monocyte/macrophage ADCC** of IgG-opsonized targets.

It is therefore a central innate immune activating receptor that links humoral (antibody) immunity to cellular cytotoxicity, and it is the key effector exploited by **therapeutic monoclonal antibodies** for tumor killing. In addition, it can participate in **antibody-dependent enhancement (ADE)** of Dengue virus infection by promoting viral entry into myeloid cells when non-neutralizing afucosylated IgG1–virus complexes are bound.

**Summary:** An NK cell/myeloid activating IgG Fc receptor (FcγRIIIa/CD16a) that binds antigen–IgG immune complexes via two Ig domains and triggers ADCC, degranulation, and cytokine responses through ITAM-containing adapter proteins.

**Function: Vimentin (class-III intermediate filament protein, cytoskeletal)**
**Function: Vimentin (class-III intermediate filament protein, cytoskeletal)**

This 466-residue protein is **vimentin**, identified with 100% sequence identity to UniProt P20152 (mouse vimentin). Its domain architecture is the classic intermediate filament (IF) signature:

- **N-terminal head domain** (residues ~7–101; Pfam Filament_head, PF04732) — enriched in Ser/Arg, a regulatory region whose phosphorylation (e.g., by PKC, CDKs, Aurora-B) controls filament assembly/disassembly.
- **Central α-helical rod domain** (residues ~102–410; Pfam Filament, PF00038, e=3e-113) — contains the coiled-coil heptad repeats (visible as the periodic E/Q/R/L residues) that mediate dimerization and higher-order assembly into 10-nm intermediate filaments.
- **C-terminal tail domain** — non-helical, involved in lateral filament interactions and cross-talk with other cytoskeletal elements.

**Biological role:** Vimentin is the major type-III IF protein of **mesenchymal cells** (fibroblasts, endothelial cells, leukocytes). It provides mechanical integrity to the cytoplasm and acts as a dynamic scaffold that:
- maintains cell shape, plasticity, and tissue integrity;
- anchors and positions the nucleus, ER, and mitochondria;
- supports cell migration, directional movement, and wound healing (including leading-edge Golgi polarization and cell-sheet organization);
- scaffolds signaling/adaptor proteins (e.g., protects SCRIB from proteasomal degradation);
- binds RNA and, together with LARP6, stabilizes type I collagen mRNAs (CO1A1/CO1A2), coupling the cytoskeleton to biosynthetic output.

It localizes mainly to the **cytoplasm/cytoskeleton** (and nuclear matrix/cell membrane in specific contexts), and it is not a transmembrane protein (no true TM segments). Overall, vimentin is a multifunctional structural and scaffolding protein that integrates mechanical support with signaling, migration, and mRNA regulation in mesenchymal cells.

**Functional prediction: Human TTK/Mps1 — dual-specificity protein kinase (UniProt P33981, 857 aa)**
**Function:** TTK/Mps1 is the kinase that initiates and sustains the mitotic spindle assembly checkpoint, delaying anaphase until all chromosomes are correctly bioriented on the mitotic spindle. Mechanistically it:
- **Phosphorylates MAD1L1**, promoting SAC signaling at unattached kinetochores;
- **Phosphorylates CDCA8/Borealin**, enhancing Aurora B kinase activity at kinetochores;
- **Phosphorylates SKA3 (Ser-34)**, promoting dissociation of the SKA complex from microtubules and destabilizing incorrect kinetochore–microtubule attachments;
- **Phosphorylates KNL1 and KNTC1** and **autophosphorylates**, coordinating kinetochore localization and checkpoint signaling.

**Cellular context:** It is a soluble (non-transmembrane) protein that shuttles between cytoplasm and nucleus and localizes to kinetochores, spindle and spindle poles during mitosis; it is essential for faithful chromosome segregation and for repair of erroneous kinetochore–microtubule attachments. Because of its role in cell division, TTK/Mps1 is an important oncology target, and its inhibitors are being explored as anti-cancer therapeutics.

**Summary:** TTK/Mps1 — a dual-specificity mitotic kinase that functions as the master regulator of the spindle assembly checkpoint, ensuring genomic stability by coupling mitotic timing and error correction to chromosome biorientation.

**Functional prediction: Mitogen-activated protein kinase 1 (MAPK1/ERK2), EC 2.7.11.24**
**Biological function:**
- ERK2 is the effector kinase of the **Ras–Raf–MEK–ERK (MAPK/ERK) pathway**, activated downstream of growth factor receptors (EGFR, KIT/SCF, insulin/IGF receptors), and relays mitogenic and stress signals to the nucleus and cytosol.
- Once activated by dual phosphorylation on the TEY motif, it phosphorylates a broad panel (>160) of substrates on Ser/Thr residues followed by Pro, including:
  - **Transcription factors** (ELK1, FOS, ATF2, ERF, BCL6, HSF4) to drive gene expression programs for proliferation and differentiation;
  - **Cytoskeletal/structural proteins** (MAPT/tau, MAP2, STMN1, paxillin, cortactin) to regulate cytoskeletal reorganization and cell motility;
  - **Apoptosis regulators** (BAD, CASP9, MCL1, DAPK1) to promote survival;
  - **Translation regulators** (EIF4EBP1, FXR1) and **downstream kinases/phosphatases** (RSKs, MNKs, MSKs, MKPs/DUSPs) that propagate and fine-tune the signal.
- It also regulates endosomal/lysosomal dynamics, Golgi fragmentation during mitosis, and the spindle assembly checkpoint; it can act as a transcriptional repressor for interferon-γ-induced genes (kinase-independent).

**Physiological relevance:** Through these activities, ERK2 controls cell growth, survival, adhesion, differentiation, meiosis/mitosis, and long-term synaptic plasticity. It is a major oncology target, as constitutive activation of the MAPK/ERK cascade (e.g., via Ras/BRAF mutations) drives many cancers; it is also central to immune signaling, development, and tissue morphogenesis.

This protein is an **ASF1-like histone chaperone**.
**Predicted function:** It is a soluble, nuclear/chromatin-associated protein (~224 aa) whose N-terminal conserved domain (residues ~11–152) belongs to the ASF1 histone chaperone family (Pfam PF04729, E = 8.5e-22) and is most closely related to histone chaperone ASF1A (UniProt Q9CQE6, 31.7% identity, E = 7e-10). Its C-terminal ~70 residues form a highly charged, likely disordered tail typical of ASF1 proteins, which mediates interactions with partner chaperone complexes (CAF-1, HIRA) and regulatory factors.

**Biological role:** It binds histone H3–H4 dimers and shuttles/deposits them to promote nucleosome assembly, histone exchange and removal. It participates in:
- **Replication-dependent chromatin assembly** via cooperation with CAF-1;
- **Replication-independent (replacement) chromatin assembly** via cooperation with HIRA;
- **DNA repair**, specifically promoting homologous-recombination repair of double-strand breaks at stalled/collapsed replication forks by mediating histone replacement and recruiting the MMS22L–TONSL complex/RAD51;
- Histone nuclear import with importin-4, recognizing newly synthesized H3/H4 with specific acetylation/methylation marks;
- Formation of senescence-associated heterochromatin foci (SAHF).

**Subcellular location:** Nucleus / chromatin (including sites of double-strand breaks). No transmembrane segments are predicted, consistent with a soluble chromatin factor.

In short: **an ASF1-family histone chaperone that regulates chromatin assembly, histone dynamics, and genome stability through nucleosome assembly/disassembly and DNA-damage-responsive chromatin remodeling.**

**Protein: MAPK3 / ERK1 (Mitogen-activated protein kinase 3, extracellular signal-regulated kinase 1)**
**Function:**
ERK1 is the central effector of the **Ras–Raf–MEK–ERK (MAPK/ERK) signal transduction pathway**. Upon growth factor, cytokine, or mitogen stimulation, it is phosphorylated/activated by MEK1/2, then phosphorylates **~160 substrates** on Ser/Thr-Pro motifs, including:
- **Transcription factors** (ELK1, FOS, ATF2, BCL6, ERF, etc.) — driving gene-expression programs for proliferation and differentiation;
- **Cytoskeletal/architecture regulators** (MAP2, MAPT, CTTN, STMN1, PXN);
- **Apoptosis regulators** (BAD, CASP9, MCL1);
- **Translational regulators** (EIF4EBP1) and downstream kinases (RSK, MNK, MSK, MAPKAPK).

Through these, ERK1 controls cell growth, survival, differentiation, adhesion, migration, meiosis/mitosis, synaptic plasticity, and endosomal/Golgi dynamics. It shuttles between cytoplasm and nucleus, with its localization/targeting modulated by partners such as PEA15. It is Mg²⁺-dependent and uses ATP to phosphorylate protein Ser/Thr residues. Because of its central role in mitogenic signaling, ERK1/ERK2 are also important oncology drug targets and biomarkers in cancer.

**Bottom line:** ERK1/MAPK3 — a proline-directed Ser/Thr MAP kinase that transduces mitogenic signals from the plasma membrane to nuclear/cytosolic effectors, governing proliferation, differentiation, and survival.

**Protein identification:** The query sequence is **F-box/LRR-repeat protein 2 (FBXL2)** — it matches mouse FBXL2 (UniProt Q8BH16) at 100% identity over the full 423 residues, so the functional assignment is essentially definitive.
**Predicted function:**
FBXL2 is the calcium/calmodulin-regulated **substrate-recognition subunit of SCF(FBXL2)**, an E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase that tags target proteins with polyubiquitin for proteasomal degradation (or monoubiquitin for regulatory turnover). Unlike many F-box proteins, it recognizes **calmodulin-binding motifs** rather than phosphodegrons, and calmodulin binding antagonizes its activity. Key validated substrates/processes include:
- **Cyclins D2/D3** — degradation promotes **G0 cell-cycle arrest**;
- **PIK3R2** — regulation of PI3K signaling and **autophagy**;
- **PCYT1A** — control of **phosphatidylcholine synthesis** and pulmonary surfactant production;
- **TRAF1–TRAF6** — negative regulation of **inflammatory signaling**;
- **NLRP3** — ubiquitination and degradation that **negatively regulates NLRP3 inflammasome assembly/activation**.

**Subcellular localization:** Membrane-associated via a lipid anchor (consistent with the weak N-terminal signal/lipid-anchor heuristic and absence of a true transmembrane domain in the TMbed prediction).

**Summary:** FBXL2 is a modular **SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase substrate adaptor** whose F-box domain recruits SKP1 and whose LRR domain binds calcium/calmodulin-sensitive substrates, coupling ubiquitin-proteasome-dependent degradation to the regulation of the cell cycle, PI3K/autophagy, phospholipid metabolism, inflammation, and the NLRP3 inflammasome.

**Protein identification:** The sequence is **human LRRC8A** (also known as **SWELL1**, UniProt Q8IWT6), a 810-amino-acid integral membrane protein. The match is definitive: 100% sequence identity to LRRC8A across the full length (E=0).
**Molecular function:** LRRC8A is the **essential/obligate subunit of the volume-regulated anion channel (VRAC, also called VSOAC)**. It assembles into hexameric channels together with other LRRC8 family members (LRRC8B–E); LRRC8A is required for channel activity, while subunit composition tunes channel properties. The channel conducts:
- **Anions** (Cl⁻ > I⁻) and **organic osmolytes** (taurine, myo-inositol) for regulatory volume decrease in response to osmotic swelling;
- **Amino acids** such as aspartate and glutamate (excitotoxic efflux under stress);
- The anticancer drug **cisplatin** (in complex with LRRC8D), relevant to chemoresistance;
- The innate-immune second messenger **2′,3′-cGAMP** (both import and export), promoting intercellular STING-pathway signaling after cytosolic DNA sensing.

**Biological roles:** Cell volume homeostasis and response to osmotic stress; B-cell (pro-B → pre-B) and T-cell development; myoblast differentiation and insulin-stimulated glucose metabolism; glucose sensing in pancreatic β-cells (VRAC activation depolarizes cells, enhancing insulin secretion); lysosomal VRAC function supporting lysosome-derived vacuole formation and cytosolic water homeostasis.

**In summary:** This protein is the pore-forming/essential subunit of the volume-regulated anion channel LRRC8A (SWELL1), a multi-pass membrane channel with a pannexin-like transmembrane pore and a cytoplasmic leucine-rich repeat domain, which mediates osmotically triggered anion/organic-osmolyte efflux, drug and cGAMP transport, and thereby governs cell volume regulation, immune signaling, and metabolic/developmental processes.

This protein is **OXI1 (OXIDATIVE SIGNAL-INDUCIBLE 1)**, a plant **serine/threonine protein kinase** (EC 2.7.11.1; AGC-family/AGCVIII) from *Arabidopsis thaliana* (UniProt Q9LSF1). It is a soluble, cytoplasmic signaling kinase whose N-terminal catalytic domain (residues ~18–178) contains all conserved protein kinase motifs — the Gly-rich ATP-binding loop, the essential catalytic lysine (VAIK), the HRD catalytic loop, and the DFG/APE activation segment — while the long C-terminal tail is Ser/Thr-rich and low-complexity, likely acting as a regulatory/phosphorylation and protein-interaction module.
**Biological function:** OXI1 is a central component of **oxidative burst–mediated signaling**. It is activated by reactive oxygen species (hydrogen peroxide) and functions as a convergence point in stress and developmental signaling. Specifically, it:
- Is **required for basal/innate resistance** to the oomycete pathogen *Peronospora parasitica* (i.e., plant defense/immune signaling);
- Is **partly required for activation of the MAP kinases MPK3 and MPK6** in response to H₂O₂ and cellulase elicitor, placing it upstream of the MAPK cascade in oxidative stress signaling;
- Is **required for root hair growth/development**, linking ROS signaling to cell morphogenesis;
- Participates in responses to oxidative stress, wounding, and hypoxia, and can positively regulate programmed cell death/apoptotic processes.

In short, OXI1 is an **ROS-activated Ser/Thr kinase that transduces oxidative stress signals into defense gene activation and developmental responses** by phosphorylating downstream substrates (including MPK3/MPK6 activation) and is a key hub coupling redox status to plant immunity and root-hair growth.

**Functional prediction: This protein is Pin1 (Peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase NIMA-interacting 1), a phosphorylation-dependent peptidyl-prolyl cis/trans isomerase (PPIase; EC 5.2.1.8).**
**Biological function:**
Pin1 acts as a **post-phosphorylation molecular switch**. It recognizes proteins that have been phosphorylated on Ser/Thr-Pro sites (e.g., by CDKs, MAPKs, and other Pro-directed kinases) and catalyzes the cis/trans isomerization of the adjacent proline bond, inducing local conformational changes that alter substrate activity, stability, localization, or protein–protein interactions. It thereby controls diverse processes including:
- **Mitosis/cell cycle** (via NIMA/NEK kinases, centrosome integrity, cytokinesis);
- **Signaling pathways** (RAF1/MEK/ERK, JNK–JUN, canonical Wnt, BTK);
- **Protein stability/degradation** (targeting PML, BCL6, FBXW7 for ubiquitin-mediated degradation; facilitating CUL3/KLHL15-dependent degradation of CtIP/RBBP8);
- **DNA damage response** (biasing repair toward error-prone NHEJ by down-regulating homologous recombination);
- **Transcription** (isomerization of RNA polymerase II CTD heptad repeats);
- **Hypoxia and inflammation** (e.g., isomerization of PGK1 and IRAK3/IRAK-M).

Given these activities, Pin1 is also relevant to oncogenesis (it can transactivate oncogenes and promote chromosome instability), neurodegeneration (regulation of amyloid-β formation and tau), and immune/inflammatory responses. In short, the protein is a **phosphorylation-dependent prolyl isomerase (parvulin-type) that functions as a central regulator of cell signaling, the cell cycle, and protein fate decisions**.

**Functional prediction: Rab5 — a small GTPase master regulator of early endocytic membrane trafficking**
**Molecular function:** Like all small GTPases, it acts as a **molecular switch**, cycling between an active **GTP-bound** and an inactive **GDP-bound** state (GTPase activity, EC 3.6.5.2). The switch is driven by the conserved G-domain motifs visible in the sequence: the P-loop `GESAVGKS` (G1, phosphate binding), `DTAGQ` (G3/Switch II, GTP hydrolysis), `GNK` (G4, guanine specificity), and `SAK` (G5). In the active state, Rab5 recruits effector proteins (e.g., EEA1, Rabaptin-5, Vps34/PI3-kinase complexes) to orchestrate membrane tethering and fusion.

**Biological role:** Rab5 is the **gatekeeper of the endocytic pathway**. It controls:
- **Clathrin-mediated/receptor-mediated endocytosis** and formation of early endosomes;
- **Homotypic early endosome fusion** and endosome maturation (including the Rab5→Rab7 conversion during progression to late endosomes/lysosomes);
- **Synaptic vesicle recycling** and maintenance of synaptic vesicle size at presynaptic terminals;
- **Developmental signaling**, e.g., establishment of morphogen (Dpp/TGF-β) gradients, Notch receptor processing, and endosomal trafficking during oogenesis (yolk protein uptake) and nervous system development.

**Subcellular localization:** Cytosol (inactive GDP form) and peripheral association with **early endosomes, multivesicular bodies, and synaptic vesicles** (active GTP form), mediated by its C-terminal lipid modification.

In summary, this protein is **Rab5**, a key regulator of vesicular transport that governs the early steps of endocytosis and endosome biogenesis, thereby coupling membrane trafficking to cell signaling, development, and neuronal function.

**Protein identity:** Human **Integrin alpha-5 (ITGA5, CD49e)** — UniProt P08648 (100% sequence identity over the full 1049-residue query).
**Biological function:** ITGA5 is the α subunit of the **integrin α5β1 (VLA-5) heterodimer**, the principal **fibronectin receptor**. It:
- mediates **RGD-dependent cell adhesion** to fibronectin (and fibrinogen, fibrillin-1);
- supports **cell–matrix adhesion, migration, proliferation, and survival** (anti-apoptotic/anoikis regulation);
- contributes to **angiogenesis, wound healing, and integrin-mediated signaling** (focal adhesion signaling, cooperation with growth factor receptors such as VEGFR and PDGFR);
- participates in cell–cell adhesion and immune/cell-surface signaling (e.g., IL-1β and CD40–CD40LG pathways);
- functions as a **virus receptor** (e.g., human metapneumovirus, parvovirus B19).

**Summary:** The protein is **integrin alpha-5**, a cell-surface adhesion/signaling receptor that dimerizes with β1 integrin to bind RGD motifs in fibronectin, governing cell adhesion, migration, angiogenesis, and tissue remodeling, and serving as a point of entry for certain pathogens.

**Protein identity: Integrin alpha-V (ITGAV / CD51), human**
**Biological function:**
Integrin αV is the shared α subunit that pairs with **β1, β3, β5, β6, and β8** to form heterodimeric cell-adhesion receptors (e.g., αVβ3/vitronectin receptor, αVβ5, αVβ6, αVβ8). These receptors mediate **cell–matrix and cell–cell adhesion** by recognizing the **RGD tripeptide** in ligands such as **vitronectin, fibronectin, fibrinogen, osteopontin, thrombospondin, laminin, vWF, prothrombin, MMP-2, and fibrillin**. Through these interactions, ITGAV integrins control:
- **Cell adhesion, spreading, migration, and cytoskeletal organization** (focal adhesion assembly);
- **Angiogenesis, vasculogenesis, and wound healing**;
- **TGF-β activation** (αVβ6/αVβ8 release active TGF-β1 from LAP), linking it to immune regulation and fibrosis;
- Signal transduction (e.g., ERK1/2, small GTPase, and calcium signaling), and in complexes such as αVβ3 it also serves as a co-receptor/partner for growth factors (FGF, NRG1, IGF, IL-1β) and fractalkine (CX3CL1).

In short, this is the **cell-surface receptor subunit integrin αV (CD51)** — a type I transmembrane, RGD-binding cell-adhesion molecule whose ectodomain β-propeller assembles with a β-integrin partner to mediate adhesion-dependent signaling in development, angiogenesis, immune function, and tissue remodeling.

This protein is **Cdc42 (cell division control protein 42 homolog)**, a Rho-family small GTPase (191 aa). It is the rat/mouse Cdc42 (UniProt Q8CFN2, 100% identity).
**Function:** Cdc42 is a plasma-membrane-associated molecular switch that cycles between an active GTP-bound state and an inactive GDP-bound state. It is a bona fide GTPase (EC 3.6.5.2) that hydrolyzes GTP to GDP + Pi, and in its active form binds a variety of effectors (e.g., WASP/WAVE, PAK kinases) to regulate:
- **Actin cytoskeleton organization**, especially the assembly of filopodia, lamellipodia and stress fibers;
- **Cell polarity** (apical/basal and front-rear polarity, epithelial polarization, neuronal polarization);
- **Cell migration, adhesion and cell–cell junction formation**;
- **Mitosis/cytokinesis**, including bipolar attachment of spindle microtubules to kinetochores and spindle orientation;
- **Neuronal plasticity** (dendritic spine formation and synapse structural plasticity, downstream of CaMKII/DOCK11);
- **Endocytosis/phagocytosis** and vesicular trafficking.

**Domain/sequence features:** The protein contains the conserved Ras-family GTPase domain (Pfam PF00071) with the hallmark P-loop (`GDGAVGKTC`), switch-II `DTAGQED` motif, and guanine-recognition region. It has no transmembrane helices; instead, it is a **peripheral membrane protein anchored to the cytoplasmic face of membranes via a C-terminal CAAX prenylation motif (`…SRRCVLL`)**, which is geranylgeranylated. Its activity is regulated by GEFs (activation), GAPs (inactivation), and GDIs (sequestration/cycling), making it a central hub in signaling pathways controlling cytoskeletal dynamics and cell morphogenesis.

**Predicted identity:** The sequence is **human receptor-type tyrosine-protein phosphatase eta (PTPRJ; also known as DEP-1/CD148)**, UniProt **Q12913** — established by 100% sequence identity across the entire 1337-residue query (MMseqs2, E-value = 0).
**Biological function:**
PTPRJ/DEP-1/CD148 is a plasma-membrane receptor tyrosine phosphatase that **dephosphorylates phosphotyrosine residues on receptor tyrosine kinases and downstream signaling proteins**, thereby acting as a **negative regulator of growth-factor signaling**. It dephosphorylates/inactivates targets including **EGFR, PDGFRB, VEGFR2 (KDR), MET, FLT3**, as well as SRC-family kinases, MAPK1/3, and PI3K subunits, and junctional/substrate proteins such as CTNND1 (p120-catenin), TJP1 and OCLN.

Consequently, it regulates:
- **Cell adhesion, migration, proliferation and differentiation**, positively affecting cell–matrix adhesion and focal adhesion assembly;
- **Contact inhibition and negative regulation of cell growth** — it is a well-established **tumor suppressor** whose loss/downregulation promotes cancer;
- **Negative regulation of EGFR/PDGFR/VEGFR signaling**, MAPK and PI3K–AKT cascades;
- **Endothelial cell survival and angiogenesis** (via VEGFR2/KDR dephosphorylation) and control of vascular permeability;
- **Platelet formation/activation and thrombosis** (megakaryocyte and platelet biology);
- **Negative regulation of TCR signaling** in T lymphocytes (down-modulating prolonged activation by dephosphorylating PLCG1/LAT);
- **Epithelial junction barrier function** during reassembly.

**Summary:** This is **PTPRJ/CD148/DEP-1**, a single-pass transmembrane receptor protein-tyrosine phosphatase whose FNIII-containing ectodomain mediates adhesion while its cytoplasmic PTP domain restrains receptor tyrosine kinase signaling — functioning as a key regulator of cell adhesion, proliferation, migration, angiogenesis/hemostasis, and immunity, and acting as a tumor suppressor.

This protein is **SUPPRESSOR OF GAMMA RESPONSE 1 (SOG1)** from Arabidopsis thaliana (UniProt Q6NQK2), a plant-specific **NAC-family transcription factor** (449 aa) that functions as the **plant functional homolog of animal p53**.
**Biological function:**
SOG1 is a master transcriptional regulator of the plant DNA-damage response (DDR). It:
- Mediates the transcriptional response to gamma irradiation and UVB-induced genotoxic stress, acting downstream of the ATM and ATR sensor kinases (without being required for ATM activation itself);
- Promotes stem-cell death and cell-cycle arrest upon DNA damage by directly regulating target genes such as the cell-cycle inhibitors **SMR5 and SMR7**;
- Participates in the DDR system that coordinates cell-cycle arrest and DNA repair;
- Separately regulates DNA repair and cytokinin signaling, playing a key role in controlling lateral root formation under genotoxic stress;
- Localizes to the nucleus and binds DNA via its N-terminal NAC domain, with a regulatory/transactivation C-terminal region.

In short, SOG1 is the p53-equivalent of plants: a NAC-family transcription factor that senses genotoxic stress through ATM/ATR signaling and orchestrates cell-cycle arrest, stem-cell death, and DNA repair to preserve genome integrity.

**Protein identity:** This sequence is **human PIEZO2 (Piezo-type mechanosensitive ion channel component 2, UniProt Q9H5I5)** — a 2,752-residue, multi-pass plasma membrane protein that is the pore-forming subunit of the mechanically activated (MA), non-selective cation channel of the same name.
**Functional interpretation:**
PIEZO2 is a **mechanosensitive ion channel**: it converts mechanical force (membrane stretch, indentation, shear) into cation influx (Na⁺, Ca²⁺, K⁺) through a cap-motion / plug-and-latch gating mechanism. It is the principal mechanotransducer for:
- **Light touch and tactile pain sensation** in sensory neurons and Merkel cells;
- **Proprioception** (position sense) and coordinated movement;
- **Airway stretch sensing** (breathing control at birth and in adults);
- **Bladder/urinary tract stretch sensing** and micturition reflexes;
- Inner-ear hair-cell mechanotransduction (as part of the MET channel complex, with PIEZO1);
- Regulation of adipose tissue, systemic metabolism, and other stretch-dependent physiological processes.

In summary, this is a large, pore-forming, mechanically gated cation channel of the Piezo family — specifically **PIEZO2** — essential for touch, pain, proprioception, and visceral mechanosensation, operating as a homotrimeric membrane-embedded force sensor.

**Protein identification:** This is **ATM (Ataxia-Telangiectasia Mutated)** from *Arabidopsis thaliana* (UniProt Q9M3G7 / F4JDV3), a 3,856-residue nuclear serine/threonine protein kinase (EC 2.7.11.1) belonging to the phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase-related kinase (PIKK) family.
**Predicted function:**
ATM is the **master regulator of the DNA-damage response (DDR)** in plants. Upon genotoxic stress such as ionizing radiation, γ-irradiation, or DNA replication stalling, ATM becomes activated and phosphorylates key downstream targets, including **histone H2AX** (producing γ-H2AX/S139ph at double-strand breaks, DSBs), thereby initiating checkpoint signaling, DNA repair, and cell-cycle arrest. It:
- Promotes **double-strand break repair** (including non-homologous end joining and synthesis-dependent strand annealing);
- Regulates **transcription of repair genes** (e.g., RAD51, PARP1, LIG4);
- Controls **meiotic DSB processing/homologous chromosome pairing** and the meiotic cell cycle;
- Regulates **telomere maintenance** (activating DDR at dysfunctional telomeres while suppressing it at functional ones);
- Cooperates with RUG3 in **organellar RNA splicing** (e.g., nd2/NAD2 mRNA), a plant-specific ATM function.

**Subcellular localization:** Nucleus (soluble, non-membrane protein despite some hydrophobic stretches), where it assembles at sites of DNA damage to orchestrate the checkpoint/repair response. In summary, this protein is the central **nuclear DNA-damage checkpoint kinase ATM of Arabidopsis**, essential for genome integrity under stress and during meiosis.

This protein is **Serine/threonine-protein kinase D1 (PRKD1/PKD1)**, the rat ortholog (UniProt Q9WTQ1, 100% identity over all 918 residues).
**Molecular function:** A Ca²⁺/DAG-responsive serine/threonine protein kinase (EC 2.7.11.13, Mg²⁺-dependent) that phosphorylates protein substrates on Ser/Thr residues. The twin C1 domains bind diacylglycerol (DAG) and phorbol esters, while the PH domain binds phosphoinositides; together they drive translocation of the enzyme from the cytosol to membranes (plasma membrane, Golgi/trans-Golgi network) — a key step in its activation downstream of PKC.

**Biological roles:** PRKD1 converts transient DAG signals into sustained cellular responses. It regulates:
- Golgi/TGN structure and vesicular trafficking (e.g., via PI4KB, promoting membrane fission and secretory transport);
- cell survival through NF-κB activation and NLRP3 inflammasome regulation;
- cell migration (integrin recycling), proliferation (sustained ERK1/2 signaling), and differentiation;
- nuclear signaling by phosphorylating class II HDACs (HDAC5/HDAC7), triggering their nuclear export and thereby controlling MEF2/RUNX2-dependent transcription (relevant to cardiac hypertrophy, angiogenesis, and osteoblast differentiation);
- EGFR/JNK pathway crosstalk and Ras signaling (via RIN1), plus innate immune signaling (TLR5/flagellin response).

**Cellular context:** soluble/cytosolic protein with no transmembrane segments; it reversibly associates with membranes upon activation and is also found in the nucleus. Its kinase activity can be unleashed by caspase-3 cleavage during apoptosis, linking it to genotoxic-stress responses.

In short, this is a DAG/phorbol-ester-regulated Ser/Thr kinase (PKD family) that acts as a multifunctional signaling hub at the interface of membrane lipid signaling, vesicle transport, gene expression, and cell survival/proliferation.
